mr. Trump says his decision to withdraw  from the Paris climate Accord was based  on economics for more on this were  joined by Brian Deese current senior  fellow at the Harvard Kennedy School of  Government and former senior advisor to  President Obama Brian played a key role  in negotiating the Paris agreement he  joins us from Portland Maine Brian good  morning morning let's just start first  with the practicalities of all of this  while there has been a lot of  consternation and controversy  surrounding the president's decision it  is inherently a four-year process to get  the u.s. out of the Paris agreement what  does that mean and why was it arranged  in such a fashion well that's right and  ironically the first day that officially  the US could withdraw is the day after  the 2020 presidential election so some  time from now it was structured that way  because this agreement was met with the  long view in mind and so countries all  around the world for the first time in  history came together in common cause to  try to do something about climate change  and so the process to get there was long  and the process to withdraw was also  intended to be significant Ryan the  president said he's open to  renegotiating this is that a viable  option I think that's incoherent you  have to understand what the actual Paris  agreement is the Paris agreement is  based on voluntary commitments by  countries so nobody outside the United  States is imposing anything on our  country we set our own target we we make  our own plan  so if President Trump wants to get  serious about climate change and wants  to put forward his own strategy for how  to address this issue  he's completely free to do so under the  agreement I think this notion of  renegotiation is largely an attempt to  hide behind what is really an act of  America retreating from the world Brian  what about the fact that the president  makes the case for the economic argument  that this is going to cost American jobs  what is your response to that look I  think this is all about economics and  the truth is right now around the world  there is a race for who is going to be  the clean energy superpower of the 21st  century this hold outdated idea that  somehow we have to trade-off between  protecting our air and water  and growing our economy is just no  longer true the United States has seen  consistent job growth for the last eight  years under President Obama while  reducing our emissions more than any  other major economy so the truth is this  is about economics there are more than  three million Americans who work in  clean energy and energy efficiency  around the country in towns across the  country good construction jobs saving  people money by upgrading the efficiency  of their homes and those are the kinds  of jobs that are growing faster in the  economy right now and the kind of jobs  we should want more of so we should be  trying to position ourselves to win  those jobs in the future and win those  export opportunities in the future not  retreating into a defensive crouch and  saying that we should stand with Syria  and Nicaragua while the rest of the  world tries to get those economic  benefits Brian we're seeing some  companies and Cu not just some companies  and CEOs but some states actually make  their own commitments to the the Accords  in effect how much can they actually do  well look you're right you've seen every  major US company come out and say this  is the right thing to do and in fact  that when this administration is far to  the right of Exxon on an issue around  climate you know that something's wrong  every American business understands that  the future is moving toward cleaner  sources of energy and that's true for  governors Republican governors and  Democratic governors who have those jobs  in those industries in their states it's  true for cities too that have a lot of  the action concretely around how to  protect their citizens from the threats  of climate change so I think what you're  going to see starting early next week is  an increasing volume of voices from  governors from theirs and from CEOs  saying even if this federal  administration is stepping back we're  going to move forward we're going to  continue our plans to reduce emissions  and that will hopefully send a signal to  the world that even though this  administration isn't with the program  America can still show some leadership  on climate change Brian Deese thank you  very much for being with us this morning  have you be here
 

